---
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SectionBand from '@components/SectionBand.astro';
import Button from '@components/Button.astro';
import AlumniGallery from '@components/AlumniGallery.astro';
import { practice } from '@data/site';
import { alumni } from '@data/alumni';

const title = 'Admin and medical assistant careers';
const description =
  'A small, physician-owned dermatology practice in Dana Point, hiring front-office staff and medical assistants — permanent roles, plus a pre-PA training path. How to apply.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/join-us/">
  <!-- 1 · Hero -->
  <SectionBand as="div" class="hero">
    <div class="hero__copy" data-reveal>
      <span class="eyebrow">Join us</span>
      <h1>We’re a small, physician-owned practice, and we’re hiring.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Advanced Dermatology has been in Dana Point since 2006. It’s physician-owned and
        independent, all in one building, with a team of about seventeen. Two board-certified
        dermatologists — one a fellowship-trained Mohs surgeon who does more than 1,000 Mohs cases
        a year — and two physician assistants see patients here, and every week Dr. Moinfar reviews
        the pathology across all of them. The reasoning behind a diagnosis gets talked through
        rather than kept to one person. If you want to learn the medicine and not just get through
        the day, it’s worth working here.
      </p>
      <p class="hero__hiring">
        We’re hiring for two kinds of roles: administrative staff for the front office, and medical
        assistants for the exam rooms. Both can be long-term jobs. Some of our medical assistants
        also use the role as a step toward PA or medical school — a path we’ve supported for about
        twenty years, and one more than 100 people have taken.
      </p>
      <div class="hero__cta">
        <Button href="#apply" variant="primary">How to apply</Button>
        <Button href="#the-path" variant="secondary">The training path ↓</Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </SectionBand>

  <!-- 2 · What we're hiring for -->
  <SectionBand id="roles" tone="fog" aria-labelledby="roles-heading">
    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <h2 id="roles-heading">What we’re hiring for</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Medical assistants</strong> work in the exam rooms — rooming patients, setting up
        procedures, and assisting the physicians and PAs through the day. It’s hands-on clinical
        work from the first week. Some people build a career in the role; others use it for a year
        or two of clinical experience before PA or medical school. We hire for both.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Administrative staff</strong> run the front office — scheduling, patient
        coordination, and billing. These are long-term roles, and the work is steadier than the
        exam rooms by design.
      </p>
    </div>
  </SectionBand>

  <!-- 3 · The medical assistant role -->
  <SectionBand aria-labelledby="job-heading">
    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <h2 id="job-heading">The medical assistant role</h2>
      <p>
        A medical assistant here rooms patients and records their history, sets up and assists with
        biopsies and excisions, works alongside Dr. Lander on Mohs surgery days, assists with
        cosmetic procedures, handles patient callbacks, and documents visits in the practice’s
        record system. The room turns over quickly and the day is full.
      </p>
      <p>
        You’re given real responsibility early, which means the first few months are a lot to
        absorb. It’s a demanding job. Some people do it for a year or two and move on to a clinical
        program; others make it a career. Both are what the role is for.
      </p>
    </div>
  </SectionBand>

  <!-- 4 · Administrative roles -->
  <SectionBand id="admin" tone="fog" aria-labelledby="admin-heading">
    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <h2 id="admin-heading">Administrative roles</h2>
      <p>
        The front office is a different kind of work from the exam rooms and a different kind of
        hire. Scheduling, patient coordination, and billing keep the practice running, and those
        roles are meant to last. In a practice this size, the front desk isn’t one seat of forty —
        you know the patients who come through, and you work directly with the providers.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you want steady administrative work in a small, physician-owned practice, that’s a real
        job here. To apply, use the address below with “Administrative” in the subject line.
      </p>
    </div>
  </SectionBand>

  <!-- 5 · The training path -->
  <SectionBand id="the-path" tone="evergreen" aria-labelledby="path-heading">
    <div class="prose prose--on-dark" data-reveal>
      <h2 id="path-heading">The training path</h2>
      <p>
        For about twenty years, this practice has also been a place pre-PA and pre-med graduates
        come to get their clinical hours. The training isn’t a classroom program. You work next to
        Dr. Moinfar and the physician assistants and learn the way they did — seeing the conditions
        in person, hearing how a diagnosis gets made, doing the parts you’re cleared to do and
        watching the rest.
      </p>
      <p>
        More than 100 people have come through that way, and a number of them are physician
        assistants or physicians now. Madeline Fee and Shylie Falahati, two of the physician
        assistants on staff, both started here as medical assistants, went to PA school, and came
        back.
      </p>
      <p>
        If that’s your plan, we hire knowing it. When you’re ready to apply, you leave with
        clinical hours that hold up, a reference from the dermatologist who trained you, and help
        thinking through where to apply. Dermatology has a turnover problem; here, the people who
        leave leave for a reason we’re glad to have been part of.
      </p>
    </div>
  </SectionBand>

  <!-- 6 · Where they are now -->
  <SectionBand aria-labelledby="now-heading">
    <p class="now__intro" data-reveal>
      As alumni give us permission to include them, this is where the last twenty years have gone.
      Names and photos are added only with a signed release.
    </p>
    <AlumniGallery heading="Where they are now" entries={alumni} />
  </SectionBand>

  <!-- 7 · Who this is for -->
  <SectionBand tone="fog" aria-labelledby="fit-heading">
    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <h2 id="fit-heading">Who this is for</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>The medical assistant role</strong> fits you if you want hands-on clinical work,
        you’re reliable and quick to learn, and you’re comfortable being told when something’s
        wrong — whether you’re building a career or getting clinical hours before a program. It’s a
        harder fit if you need a slow, predictable pace and a fixed schedule; the exam rooms don’t
        work that way.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>An administrative role</strong> fits you if you’re organized, steady, and good with
        patients on the phone and at the desk, and you want to stay somewhere a while. You don’t
        need a clinical background.
      </p>
    </div>
  </SectionBand>

  <!-- 8 · How to apply -->
  <SectionBand id="apply" aria-labelledby="apply-heading">
    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <h2 id="apply-heading">How to apply</h2>
      <p>
        Email a resume and a short note — why now, and what you’re looking for — to
        <a
          class="link-underline"
          href="mailto:derm@dermsc.com?subject=Medical%20assistant%20application">derm@dermsc.com</a
        >
        with “Medical assistant” in the subject line, or “Administrative” if you’re applying for a
        front-office role. If you’d rather call, the practice number is
        <a class="link-underline" href={`tel:${practice.phoneHref}`}>{practice.phone}</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        We read every application that comes in. If it looks like a fit, you’ll come in to meet the
        team and spend time in the clinic before anyone decides.
      </p>
    </div>
  </SectionBand>

  <!-- 9 · Closing CTA -->
  <SectionBand tone="evergreen-deep" aria-labelledby="cta-heading">
    <div class="closing" data-reveal>
      <h2 id="cta-heading">Apply</h2>
      <p>
        Send a resume and a short note about the role you want and why now. If it’s a fit, we’ll
        bring you in to meet the team and see the clinic.
      </p>
      <div class="closing__cta">
        <Button
          href="mailto:derm@dermsc.com?subject=Application"
          variant="primary"
        >
          Email your resume
        </Button>
        <Button href={`tel:${practice.phoneHref}`} variant="secondary">
          Call {practice.phone}
        </Button>
      </div>
      <p class="closing__cross">
        Want to know how the practice is set up first?
        <a class="link-underline" href="/about/">About Advanced Dermatology</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </SectionBand>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Hero — padding-block from .section; h1 → .lede gap is global. */
  .hero__hiring {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    max-width: var(--wrap-prose);
  }

  /* Where they are now */
  .now__intro {
    max-width: var(--wrap-prose);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-12);
    color: var(--stone);
  }

  /* Closing — .closing / .closing__cta are shared; only the cross-link note is local */
  .closing__cross {
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
    color: var(--stone);
  }
</style>
